/// \file array.mla
/// \brief Fixed-capacity array reference for MLang (std::array).
///
/// `array<T, N>` is a compiler-provided fixed-capacity sequence type. It is
/// list-compatible at the method level, so normal collection methods such as
/// `len`, `is_empty`, `push`, `pop`, and `clear` are available. Capacity is
/// fixed at `N`: compile-time-sized initializers are checked by the compiler,
/// and mutating methods check capacity before writing.
///
/// Literal and fill initializers are checked by the compiler:
///
/// ```mlang
/// let fixed: array<i32, 6> = {1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
/// var scratch: array<i32, 6> = {};
/// scratch.push(10);
/// scratch.extend(vec![20, 30]);
/// scratch.pop();
///
/// var filled: array<i32, 4> = {};
/// filled.fill(7); // len is now 4
/// ```
///
/// `extend(values)` appends a compatible `list<T>`, `vec![...]`, or another
/// `array<T, M>` when the resulting length is at most `N`. If the compiler can
/// prove the result would exceed `N`, it emits a compile-time error. Unknown
/// runtime-sized sources keep a capacity guard that stops before reallocating
/// or writing past the end. Element types can also be containers, such as
/// `array<list<i32>, N>` or `array<map<str8, i32>, N>`.
///
/// Constant out-of-bounds indexes are rejected at compile time. Dynamic indexes
/// are checked before loading.
///
/// `size_of(array<T, N>)` reports fixed storage bytes: `N * size_of(T)`.

// @builtin array
// Fixed-capacity sequence type: array<T, N>.
// Initializer element counts are checked at compile time where known.
// push/extend/fill are checked so they never exceed N. Provable overflows are
// compile-time errors; unknown-sized sources keep runtime guards.
// Indexing is also checked: constant bad indexes fail at compile time and
// dynamic indexes keep runtime guards.

// Documentation-only anchor for `mod std::array;`.
extern fn array() -> void;
